\subsection{Per-Layer Action Decoding Across Training Stages}
\label{sec:action_r2_threecond}

Figure~\ref{fig:action_r2_threecond} compares per-layer action probe $R^2$ across three training stages (untrained, Llama-2 base, VLA-trained) using 50 demonstrations per task, averaged over 3 seeds (mean $\pm$ std).
All three conditions share L0 representations ($R^2 = 0.645 \pm 0.008$); subsequent differences thus arise from Transformer processing rather than input variation.
The trained model peaks at L9 ($R^2 = 0.643 \pm 0.014$), consistent with progressive specialization for action-relevant feature integration.
Llama-2 base shows a steady decline (L0: $0.645 \to$ L32: $0.551$, std $0.003$--$0.035$), indicating stable but unspecialized processing.
The untrained model exhibits high cross-seed variance and deep-layer collapse, with negative $R^2$ and bimodal behavior across seeds at late layers; this instability confirms that randomly initialized Transformer layers produce destructive interference on action-relevant structure.
The three-condition gradient (trained $>$ Llama-2 base $>$ untrained) holds from L1 through L15, with a reversal at L32 where trained $R^2$ drops below the pretrained baseline.
The qualitative pattern is consistent across all 3 seeds.

