Leave-one-out cross-validation over 10 folds (each fold holds out one task's instructions) confirms the cross-instruction probe result is not driven by memorization.
Trained last\_preaction achieves $100\% \pm 0.00$ across all 10 folds at layers L1--L31, confirming perfect generalization.

\subsubsection{QA Probe: Progress and Subtask Classification}
\label{sec:qa_probe}

To test whether LLM layers encode task-relevant semantic information beyond instruction identity, we train probes to classify task progress (early/mid/late) and active subtask from frozen representations (Table~\ref{tab:qa_probe}).

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{QA probe accuracy (progress + subtask classification, best layer).}
\label{tab:qa_probe}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
Source & Best Layer & Accuracy \\
\midrule
Trained last\_preaction & L20 & 86.3\% \\
Untrained last\_preaction & L20 & 85.3\% \\
DINOv2 cls\_token & --- & 82.7\% \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsubsection{Cross-Instruction Probe Profiles}
\label{sec:probe_profiles}

\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_probe_conditions.pdf}
\caption{Cross-instruction 10-way classification accuracy at the readout token across all 32 LLM layers. VLA-trained achieves $99.6\%$ at L1, saturating to $100\%$. Llama-2 base starts at $80.2\%$, reaching $100\%$ by L5. Untrained starts at $100\%$ but decays to $82\%$ by L31.}
\label{fig:probe_conditions_appendix}
\end{figure}

\subsubsection{L1 Attention Pattern Analysis}
\label{sec:attention_pattern}

Figure~\ref{fig:attention_appendix} shows the per-head L1 attention distribution at the readout token.
The three-level gradient (untrained 0/32, Llama-2 base 9/32, VLA-trained 24/32 instruction-dominant heads) shows that language pretraining seeds partial instruction routing and VLA fine-tuning shifts it further toward instruction dominance.

\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_l1_stacked_bar.pdf}
\caption{L1 attention from the readout token across three conditions, per head (sorted by instruction attention fraction). VLA-trained: 24/32 instruction-dominant heads ($57.7\%$ text attention). Llama-2 base: 9/32 heads ($36.5\%$). Untrained: 0/32 heads ($11.7\%$). See Table~\ref{tab:routing_gradient} for quantitative comparison.}
\label{fig:attention_appendix}
\end{figure}

\subsubsection{Multi-Layer Attention Patterns}
\label{sec:multilayer_attention}

Table~\ref{tab:multilayer_attention} shows a 4-layer subset of the full 32-layer attention profile (Figure~\ref{fig:attention_profile}, main text).
The full profile confirms three qualitatively distinct patterns: the untrained model maintains 0 instruction-dominant heads at all 32 layers; Llama-2 base shows a mid-depth peak (L9--L16: 15--22 heads), consistent with typical autoregressive LM processing depth; VLA fine-tuning produces an entry-exit pattern (L0--L1: 21--24 heads, L31: 18 heads, intermediate layers 0--11).

\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_multi_layer_attn.pdf}
\caption{Instruction-dominant heads and mean text attention fraction across layers for three conditions. VLA-trained shows elevated values at L1 and L31; Llama-2 base peaks at L16; untrained remains near zero throughout.}
\label{fig:multilayer_attention}
\end{figure}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Instruction-dominant heads and text attention fraction at four sampled layers (subset of the 32-layer profile in Figure~\ref{fig:attention_profile}). VLA-trained shows entry-exit concentration (L1, L31); Llama-2 base peaks at mid-depth (L16); untrained shows no instruction routing. Head counts from the 32-layer bulk extraction may differ slightly from dedicated per-layer analyses (Table~\ref{tab:routing_gradient}) due to threshold boundary effects.}
\label{tab:multilayer_attention}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}
\toprule
 & \multicolumn{2}{c}{VLA Trained} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{Llama-2 base} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{Untrained} \\
\cmidrule(lr){2-3} \cmidrule(lr){4-5} \cmidrule(lr){6-7}
Layer & Heads & Text\% & Heads & Text\% & Heads & Text\% \\
\midrule
L1 & \textbf{24/32} & 57.7 & 9/32 & 36.5 & 0/32 & 11.7 \\
L8 & 4/32 & 46.8 & 8/32 & 48.8 & 0/32 & 7.9 \\
L16 & 10/32 & 48.4 & \textbf{25/32} & 60.2 & 0/32 & 9.2 \\
L31 & \textbf{18/32} & 56.3 & 8/32 & 34.9 & 0/32 & 7.6 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsubsection{Threshold Sensitivity Analysis}
\label{sec:threshold_sensitivity}

The main text defines instruction-dominant heads as those allocating $>50\%$ attention to instruction tokens.
Table~\ref{tab:threshold_sensitivity} shows that the three-condition gradient (untrained $<$ Llama-2 base $<$ VLA-trained) holds across all thresholds from 30\% to 70\%, confirming that the 50\% threshold is not a boundary-sensitive choice.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Number of instruction-dominant heads at L1 under varying attention thresholds. The three-condition ordering is stable across all thresholds.}
\label{tab:threshold_sensitivity}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
Threshold & VLA Trained & Llama-2 base & Untrained \\
\midrule
30\% & 32 & 13 & 0 \\
40\% & 31 & 10 & 0 \\
50\% & 24 & 9 & 0 \\
60\% & 9 & 4 & 0 \\
70\% & 5 & 3 & 0 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

